- **`--keep-alive`** — Holds the GrantivaAgent session open after flows complete. The app stays frozen in whatever state the flow left it. Ctrl-C (or `kill -INT` on a backgrounded run, or `grantiva runner stop` from the same directory) releases it, reaping grantiva-runner, WebDriverAgent, and any diagnostics they started. A release once the hold is armed (`Press Ctrl-C to release.` printed) ends the run with the status its flows earned: the flow blocks and `Flows:` summary print, and it exits 0 for a pass, 1 for a failure (with the `Error:` line), as without `--keep-alive`; the ready file keeps the `passed` or `failed` it wrote. A stop during the flows, or a second Ctrl-C during a release, interrupts the run: exit 130, ready file `interrupted`.
- **`--ready-file <path>`** — Writes that file once, atomically, when the run reaches a terminal state. With `--keep-alive` the session deliberately outlives the flows, so process exit is not a completion signal and `report.json` is rewritten incrementally; wait on this file instead:

  ```bash
  grantiva run --flow flows/advertise.yaml --keep-alive --ready-file /tmp/advertise.ready &
  while [ ! -f /tmp/advertise.ready ]; do sleep 0.2; done
  jq -r .status /tmp/advertise.ready   # passed | failed | interrupted
  ```

  Two guarantees make that loop safe. The file is **deleted at startup**, before any project, build, or simulator work, so a file left by a previous run can never be read as this one's verdict — and an unwritable path fails immediately rather than at the end of a long suite. And it is **always written**: a failure before the runner starts (no project, bad scheme, build failure, no simulator) records `failed` rather than leaving the loop, which has no timeout, spinning until CI's global limit; a usage error such as `--timeout 5` records `failed` with an `error` field.

  Missing parent directories of the path are created (`--ready-file out/ci/x.ready` creates `out/ci/`), so a typo in the directory part makes a new directory rather than failing; only an uncreatable or unwritable location is an error.

  `status` is `interrupted` after Ctrl-C (or `kill -INT`/`-TERM`) and `failed` after a `--timeout` kill; either way a flow the run never finished is listed as `interrupted` or `failed`, never `running`. `reportDir` is present whenever `--report-dir` was given and the directory exists, pass or fail: the default report directory is deleted when the run exits. When a run has both `screens:` and `flows:`, `flows` lists the screens session as `screens` ahead of the flows.
- **`--env KEY=VALUE`** — Sets an environment variable for the app under test (repeatable). Forwarded through the flow's `launchApp` (`environment:` on iOS, `arguments:` intent extras on Android), so an ephemeral port or test fixture can be passed in per run.
- **`grantiva hierarchy`** — Reads the current UI accessibility tree of the running app via the held session. Pure read, no relaunch, no state loss. XML (default) or JSON. Finds the newest live `--keep-alive` session in `/tmp/grantiva-sessions/`, or a specific device's with `--udid <UDID or adb serial>`; sessions whose runner has exited are ignored. See [docs/dump-hierarchy.md](docs/dump-hierarchy.md).
- **Concurrent runs** — Runs on different simulator UDIDs execute in parallel. Each simulator launches WebDriverAgent from its own runner home (`<runner home>/versions/<stamp>/devices/<udid>`, where the runner home is `GRANTIVA_RUNNER_HOME` or `~/.grantiva/runner` and `<stamp>` is the installed runner's stamp: the runner version plus the bundled Android driver tag, such as `1.1.28-grantiva.15+android-drivers-2`), which holds an APFS clone of the cached WDA build, so every run gets its own xctestrun, port, and DerivedData without rebuilding WDA. `simulator teardown` (with or without `--force`) and `simulator delete` remove the device's home under every installed `versions/<stamp>/`, and teardown exits non-zero naming any home it could not remove; homes of simulators that no longer exist are pruned on the next run. A second run targeting an already-owned simulator fails immediately with guidance to provision a unique simulator, protecting the active WDA session from cross-run teardown; when the owner is a `grantiva runner start` session the message says so and names `grantiva runner stop`. A run's lease file (`<runner home>/locks/<udid>.lock`) is removed when the run ends, and a deleted simulator's when it is deleted.
- **`--logs`** — Streams app logs from the simulator or emulator, prefixed with `[log]` and interleaved with the flow output. On iOS (`xcrun simctl spawn log stream`) the predicate is scoped to your app: subsystems starting with its bundle ID, plus every line from its process (the installed app's `CFBundleExecutable`); override it with `--logs-predicate`. On Android `logcat` is filtered to the app's uid; `--logs-tag <tag>` keeps one tag and implies `--logs`. `--logs-level default|info|debug` sets the level on both (see [docs/android.md](docs/android.md#logs)).
- **`--logs-predicate '<NSPredicate>'`** — Custom log filter (iOS) for narrowing to specific subsystems, categories, or processes. Implies `--logs`.
OVERVIEW: Run Maestro flows against a simulator or emulator. No visual
regression — reports step pass/fail and captures a screenshot on failure.

USAGE: grantiva run <options>

OPTIONS:
  --json                  Output as JSON
  --verbose               Print diagnostic detail (timestamps, labels,
                          metadata) to stderr.
  --quiet                 Silence progress diagnostics on stderr; warnings and
                          errors still print. Program output on stdout is
                          unaffected.
  --app-file <app-file>   Path to a pre-built app: .app bundle or .ipa archive
                          (iOS), .apk (Android). Skips the build step.
  --no-build              Skip building and installing — assume the app is
                          already on the device. With --app-file, that file is
                          installed (only the build is skipped).
  --derived-data-path <derived-data-path>
                          Write Xcode build products and intermediates to this
                          DerivedData directory.
  --platform <platform>   Target platform: ios or android. Defaults to
                          whichever of grantiva.yml / grantiva-android.yml
                          exists, else the project files in this directory.
                          GRANTIVA_PLATFORM also sets it. (values: ios, android)
  --scheme <scheme>       Scheme to build (iOS)
  --simulator <simulator> Simulator name or UDID (iOS)
  --bundle-id <bundle-id> Bundle identifier (iOS)
  --module <module>       Gradle module to assemble (Android; default app)
  --variant <variant>     Gradle build variant, e.g. debug or freeDebug
                          (Android; default debug)
  --application-id <application-id>
                          Application ID (Android; read from the build or the
                          APK when omitted)
  --emulator <emulator>   AVD name to use, booting it if needed (Android)
  --device <device>       adb serial of an attached emulator or physical device
                          (Android); for a build, its ABI picks the split APK
  --allow-device-settings Apply demo mode and animation settings on a physical
                          device too (Android)
  --headless              Boot an emulator without a window (Android).
                          GRANTIVA_EMULATOR_HEADLESS=1 or CI=true does the
                          same; otherwise a booted emulator gets a window, even
                          when stdout is not a terminal.
  --flow <flow>           Run a single flow file instead of all configured flows
  --keep-alive            Keep the runner session (GrantivaAgent on iOS,
                          UIAutomator2 on Android) alive after flows complete
                          so `grantiva hierarchy` can inspect UI state without
                          relaunching the app. Release with Ctrl-C.
  --logs                  Stream app logs from the simulator or emulator into
                          this terminal, prefixed with [log] (iOS and Android).
                          On iOS the filter defaults to lines whose subsystem
                          starts with the app's bundle ID or whose process is
                          the app's executable; on Android, to lines from the
                          app's uid.
  --logs-predicate <logs-predicate>
                          Custom NSPredicate for `simctl log stream
                          --predicate` (iOS). Implies --logs.
  --logs-tag <logs-tag>   logcat tag to keep when streaming logs (Android).
                          Implies --logs.
  --logs-level <logs-level>
                          Log level for --logs: default, info, debug. Defaults
                          to `default`. iOS passes it to `log stream --level`;
                          on Android default and info keep logcat priority I
                          and above, debug adds D. (values: default, info,
                          debug)
  --snapshot <snapshot>   Snapshot policy: failure (default — one shot after
                          failure), trailing (last-good step + failure step),
                          full (every step). (values: failure, trailing, full;
                          default: failure)
  --continue-on-failure   Keep running remaining flows after a failure. Default
                          is fail-fast — stop the suite on the first broken
                          flow, which matches CI semantics and avoids wasting
                          cycles.
  --report-dir <report-dir>
                          Write the runner's report.json + assets to this
                          directory (workspace-relative). Survives grantiva's
                          cleanup so CI can upload it. Default: ephemeral tmp
                          dir.
  --timeout <timeout>     Max seconds to wait for the runner subprocess before
                          killing it with SIGTERM. Default: 600 (10 min).
                          Minimum 30. Ignored under --keep-alive. Bump this for
                          long multi-flow suites. (default: 600)
  --ready-file <ready-file>
                          Write this file once the run reaches a terminal
                          state, containing its status. Deleted at startup, and
                          always written — a setup failure records `failed`
                          rather than leaving a waiter hanging. Missing parent
                          directories are created. Wait on it with `while [ !
                          -f <path> ]; do sleep 0.2; done` instead of polling
                          report.json — useful with --keep-alive, where the
                          session outlives the flows.
  --env <env>             Environment variable for the app under test, as
                          KEY=VALUE. Repeatable. Forwarded through the flow's
                          launchApp (environment on iOS, string intent extras
                          on Android).
  --version               Show the version.
  -h, --help              Show help information.

